#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee9e1b8-e775-53bb-80f5-dff546488c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717024} {:answer/id #uuid "3083d1e1-7d60-5599-9ebc-6db5a4c13ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717025} {:answer/id #uuid "a668d0f2-c9bd-53be-9b71-03bd9a405c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717029} {:answer/id #uuid "e65b854b-6beb-5cae-9977-68abd6afa736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717034} {:answer/id #uuid "fb813e9a-5cad-5a5c-800a-488412de91ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717036}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717029} :answer-field/id #uuid "71b663c8-6525-5072-9c57-d53597e690ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "58b4f58f-3eb0-5412-8367-014189c2c54c" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2,-1,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 1,2,2\\rangle$. Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "The vector we project onto is $\\mathbf{b}$, so its magnitude is the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}\n&=\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}}{\\lVert\\mathbf{b}\\rVert}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2(1)+(-1)(2)+1(2)}{\\sqrt{1^2+2^2+2^2}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2-2+2}{\\sqrt{9}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae3f51e5-20f9-5df1-8950-d0f82c02edf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717030}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717030} :answer-field/id #uuid "17abfda7-9b02-56d9-81fd-6dea6e44dcf7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b011c631-2c9d-57ef-ba33-eea4b885fbe8" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle t,2,-1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 2,-1,2\\rangle$, where $t$ is real. Given that $\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}=-2$, find the exact value of $\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First use the given projection to determine the missing component. Since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert\\mathbf{b}\\rVert&=\\sqrt{2^2+(-1)^2+2^2}=3,\\\\\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}&=2t+2(-1)+(-1)(2)=2t-4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2t-4}{3}&=-2,\\\\\n2t-4&=-6,\\\\\nt&=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle-1,2,-1\\rangle$. The reverse projection uses the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert\\mathbf{a}\\rVert&=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+2^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt{6},\\\\\n\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\mathbf{a}&=2(-1)+(-1)(2)+2(-1)=-6,\\\\\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}\n&=\\frac{\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\mathbf{a}}{\\lVert\\mathbf{a}\\rVert}\n=\\frac{-6}{\\sqrt{6}}\n=-\\sqrt{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReversing the projection keeps the dot product but changes the denominator, so the answer need not equal the given $-2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c5d514-a035-5282-a62b-5912d154948c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717273} {:answer/id #uuid "65af39aa-4d4e-529b-81d6-630f2d04cec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717282} {:answer/id #uuid "69cb3058-1e3b-5f10-8994-74d073c4b5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717283} {:answer/id #uuid "c132f768-760e-5dbc-add5-b142324b2515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717290} {:answer/id #uuid "f04db27a-86c7-59de-b648-a8138ed01d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717296}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717282} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ef99f4c-ac1b-5986-b86e-14de1be5bc9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "08876890-b6e2-5467-8c5b-5265445aaaf1" :question/problem "Let $b=\\langle -2,-4,3\\rangle$ and $a=\\langle 2,1,1\\rangle$. Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$." :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(-2)+(1)(-4)+(1)(3)}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-4+3}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2920f39a-f19f-5d80-9ef8-7e850d93da0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717275} {:answer/id #uuid "5391a191-70dd-5a1a-9ebb-ed1ee473de63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717280} {:answer/id #uuid "6274c9cd-b504-5b71-9259-5edd52adb837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717281} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9ddb46-cfe6-5cd5-a1c0-bceae7d9e3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717288} {:answer/id #uuid "eadcb108-bbb8-5bf6-8764-9657f99f8e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717295}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717295} :answer-field/id #uuid "d95c1088-0e96-5101-80d7-8218e1e99341" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4c5e7dcf-e551-5e0b-bcb1-99d4b11acae3" :question/problem "Let $b=\\langle -1,-2,-2\\rangle$ and $a=\\langle -1,1,-2\\rangle$. Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$." :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)(-1)+(1)(-2)+(-2)(-2)}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2+4}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "240f0757-b369-55c2-aa00-212622bfd9f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{30}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717274} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e8e56eb-c060-5eea-8938-a10157003991" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9acfdb25-0b02-5b4b-86fa-5ba4a1e13c89" :question/problem "Let $a=\\langle 0,-2\\sqrt{5},0\\rangle$ and $b=\\langle 1,-2\\sqrt{5},-3\\rangle$. Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{b}a=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)(1)+(-2\\sqrt{5})(-2\\sqrt{5})+(0)(-3)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+20+0}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{30}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12eea4eb-7288-57ff-8c3b-bf2a53b02134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717271} {:answer/id #uuid "14d9b947-587d-5d30-b3bb-a71d4a228b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\left(3-2\\sqrt{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717272} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd57dc7-c140-5065-8b25-c1514ea480ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717276} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5c4118-8df2-504d-8656-a50ddcac2bf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{13}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717284} {:answer/id #uuid "77b466ae-2d49-5bdc-96d9-e5e32dc7bb14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{13}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717285}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717271} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc791c84-bde2-5fb3-b57c-65b1b9d34188" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a04c3108-b0df-506b-8f2d-7efaa14c5306" :question/problem "Let $b=\\langle 2,2\\sqrt{2},1\\rangle$ and $a=\\langle 1,\\sqrt{2},-3\\rangle$. Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$." :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)(2)+(\\sqrt{2})(2\\sqrt{2})+(-3)(1)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+4-3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d03eee-882d-51f5-a153-b18a5c7c30a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717279}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717279} :answer-field/id #uuid "eac2e645-2297-5fb0-a4ba-10a5c5c05297" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e980fae5-696e-5f27-b57d-3b157441b2c0" :question/problem "Let $a=\\langle -3,2\\sqrt{5},1\\rangle$ and $b=\\langle -3,-\\sqrt{5},1\\rangle$. Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{b}a=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)(-3)+(2\\sqrt{5})(-\\sqrt{5})+(1)(1)}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-10+1}{\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f94d37ca-1302-50b0-9d81-5194ef3a6e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717357}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717357} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5095327-b697-528a-810d-14d6d9273d40" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0cd2770e-b734-50fd-b397-225b6ca4fad6" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\mathbf{m}|=12$ and the angle between nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{m}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ is $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{m}$ onto $\\mathbf{n}$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\mathbf{m}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the cosine of the included angle to find the signed scalar projection.\n\nWe project $\\mathbf{m}$, so we use $|\\mathbf{m}|=12$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\mathbf{m} &=|\\mathbf{m}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=12\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&=12\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar projection is positive because the included angle is acute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a068048e-ffbf-56a0-abc6-b71bb3253070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 6 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717351}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717351} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f1d39f4-d641-5f2f-989e-80ca30d96c5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "507288c7-190d-58fe-8006-cd89db648f63" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\mathbf{d}|=12$ and the angle between nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ is $\\theta=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$. Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{d}$ onto $\\mathbf{c}$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{c}}\\mathbf{d}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the cosine of the included angle to find the signed scalar projection.\n\nWe project $\\mathbf{d}$, so we use $|\\mathbf{d}|=12$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{c}}\\mathbf{d} &=|\\mathbf{d}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=12\\cos\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&=12\\cdot\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=- 6 \\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar projection is negative because the included angle is obtuse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bdf7f1e-4752-592e-896c-2c6d2e4fa553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717349} :answer-field/id #uuid "131569b7-94df-5ec4-ac2d-3dcb94620880" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f5c2d08-59d5-515a-a70c-2e419edd2e6b" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\mathbf{c}|=12 \\sqrt{3}$ and the angle between nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ is $\\theta=30^\\circ$. Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{c}$ onto $\\mathbf{d}$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\mathbf{c}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the cosine of the included angle to find the signed scalar projection.\n\nWe project $\\mathbf{c}$, so we use $|\\mathbf{c}|=12 \\sqrt{3}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\mathbf{c} &=|\\mathbf{c}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=12 \\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(30^\\circ\\right) \\\\\n&=12 \\sqrt{3}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=18.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar projection is positive because the included angle is acute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0156783c-da02-5e6c-9fca-240cec6a1eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717339} {:answer/id #uuid "23831637-408f-5f07-8890-35ffdda329c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717344} {:answer/id #uuid "43824b8a-be4f-5263-a718-3573ee0158bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717345} {:answer/id #uuid "62f75bf2-0aef-5472-8953-22b741073d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717347} {:answer/id #uuid "d312ae92-9bad-517b-a139-5e686324434b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717353}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717353} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bba8316-02af-58eb-ad87-8b133f01790f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d8078238-0c70-5aa3-911b-a2f0a489c32f" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\mathbf{v}|=7 \\sqrt{2}$ and the angle between nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ is $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$. Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{v}$ onto $\\mathbf{u}$." :question/worked-solution "Use the cosine of the included angle to find the signed scalar projection.\n\nWe project $\\mathbf{v}$, so we use $|\\mathbf{v}|=7 \\sqrt{2}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{u}}\\mathbf{v} &=|\\mathbf{v}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=7 \\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&=7 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar projection is positive because the included angle is acute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f6e501b-9563-56e2-9518-710e3301f2fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717343}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717343} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3c06d7b-c773-5c02-a750-6e5fa89350e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dfd91087-02c1-5ec6-94de-300e5a56c5bd" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\mathbf{m}|=6$ and the angle between nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{m}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ is $\\theta=120^\\circ$. Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{m}$ onto $\\mathbf{n}$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\mathbf{m}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the cosine of the included angle to find the signed scalar projection.\n\nWe project $\\mathbf{m}$, so we use $|\\mathbf{m}|=6$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\mathbf{m} &=|\\mathbf{m}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=6\\cos\\left(120^\\circ\\right) \\\\\n&=6\\cdot\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar projection is negative because the included angle is obtuse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ccf2f7c-1a69-5f73-910d-dd033f0931b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717695} {:answer/id #uuid "ca73176d-eb23-5602-a5b5-fb712c201ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717708}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717695} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a5f76af-4d42-58eb-82f6-aad57c1b898e" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717689} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28213c83-9dfa-5e18-a56e-e6e57e882673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717692} {:answer/id #uuid "3336d4f5-0299-575a-8554-cd74d0533748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717693}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717692} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a34bcfe-94ed-554c-b3f6-ccf194e1801b" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "895b97e2-da8c-5864-84e1-c60c464785ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717704} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5239ef-1f32-50b1-ab72-5d7e96338b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717705}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717704} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1b7213f-d566-580f-b7cd-f2b2dcb43c03" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5b47cdcf-3ada-5842-9ad0-10098bac59cf" :question/problem "![Vector field following elliptical streamlines and a clockwise path](images/af/af4cc83cbf80aad55631338df3d6fd7e348ef9d3eefd6b7d107712af2010e20d.png)\n\nThe gray curves are streamlines of $\\mathbf F$: the field follows these ellipses, including the blue ellipse $S$. Let $C$ traverse $S$ once clockwise, and let $-C$ denote the reversed traversal. In both cases, flux is measured with the outward-pointing normal.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Flux means the net flow through the boundary, measured using the outward-pointing unit normal $\\mathbf n$:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward flow contributes positively, inward flow negatively, and tangential flow contributes zero.\n\nAlong $S$, each field vector is tangent to the ellipse and perpendicular to its outward normal. Thus $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n=0$ at every boundary point: the flow moves along the boundary without crossing it.\n\n![Tangential field vectors and outward normals on the ellipse](images/76/7667f7e5229779efaff5694f34d926be0569e293ab47a3dc6695272fe73032b0.png)\n\nIt follows that $\\Phi_C=0$. Reversing traversal leaves the outward normals unchanged, so $\\Phi_{-C}=0$ as well. The clockwise direction of $C$ does not make outward flux negative.\n\n- The flux across $C$ is zero: **true**.\n- The flux across $-C$ is zero: **true**.\n- The flux across $C$ is negative: **false**.\n\nSelect both zero-flux statements."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b1f8209-4e4f-53ed-82bb-55d4a22b7d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717687} {:answer/id #uuid "714b5c46-5ff3-52c3-8a7c-da01ef24f476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717701}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717687} :answer-field/id #uuid "2055d29d-28a8-502c-99cd-90fd154ec70f" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dfda61d-1ab8-5bb6-9040-2eca3f2fb116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717690} {:answer/id #uuid "389eba94-36e2-587d-9ae8-90eed2db9665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717694}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717690} :answer-field/id #uuid "db39d562-0661-5c07-89d2-57704fa6ddb8" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is positive." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717712} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9b24fac-faa6-5606-a21d-27fea6a814c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717706} {:answer/id #uuid "d590bce5-d2e9-5cf7-af36-a2bb9e29749b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717711} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdc1fbf3-9fc3-5338-bc82-0c84891d7067" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c55a9600-9361-5ef0-8898-8ea9dd733ffd" :question/problem "![Inward vector field and a counterclockwise circular path](images/43/4374424f671e1e85688fc92368d0f1cdb4b3310b10bb6ee781d45936b5b0fca4.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ traverse $S$ once counterclockwise, and let $-C$ denote the reversed traversal. In both cases, flux is measured with the outward-pointing normal.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Flux means the net flow through the boundary, measured using the outward-pointing unit normal $\\mathbf n$:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward flow contributes positively, inward flow negatively, and tangential flow contributes zero.\n\nThe field points into the circle at every boundary point. Its component along the outward normal is negative, so there is net inflow and $\\Phi_C<0$.\n\n![Field vectors oppose the outward normals](images/d6/d6f1a4ddeffb19a26c2b1da8230f348e498df88451f2eb82302ab33433b68a8c.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore $\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C<0$.\n\n- The flux across $-C$ is positive: **false**.\n- The flux across $C$ is zero: **false**.\n- The flux across $C$ is negative: **true**.\n\nSelect only the statement that the flux across $C$ is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e8b9ab6-bab1-5c8d-8734-8d9d380f1dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717697} {:answer/id #uuid "ff269de3-72e9-5945-9da8-ac1bbebaa9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717715}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717715} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cb12108-5d05-5ef6-a9d4-cef714bd36a8" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717688} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70ce9c30-e890-5e2d-a08c-bf14bb8ff5d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717700} {:answer/id #uuid "d02ce198-6908-53af-9053-35914472c368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717709}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717709} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bef2037-3d45-5d72-b415-efe0588d0ab1" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717702} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d387e8-c78c-5303-8657-0324bb6d48fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717686} {:answer/id #uuid "61b76a3e-b74f-5baf-974f-f4d4628f6fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717699}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717699} :answer-field/id #uuid "82a17138-2cb1-5b4e-a412-a5bb4de62904" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fa3d02d0-6d6f-577e-8ee8-353eb4d96c34" :question/problem "![Combined uniform and circulating vector field around a circle](images/e9/e9073825b7a850bbd7744f7047ed779c18a9cf3c9f1136991fffd5b4ff465c8d.png)\n\nThe field $\\mathbf F$ is formed by adding a uniform rightward flow to a counterclockwise flow tangent to every circle centered at $O$. The blue circle $S$ is centered at $O$. The arrows show the combined field.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once counterclockwise, and let $-C$ denote the reversed traversal. In both cases, flux is measured with the outward-pointing normal.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Flux means the net flow through the boundary, measured using the outward-pointing unit normal $\\mathbf n$:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward flow contributes positively, inward flow negatively, and tangential flow contributes zero.\n\nConsider the two component flows separately. The outward component of their sum is the sum of their outward components.\n\n![Uniform flow crosses opposite arcs equally; circulating flow is tangent](images/39/397d4353134f6638059fadddcbad3e0bdbc210a07cdeea10d93bdf36c8c2afa0.png)\n\nThe circulating component is tangent to $S$, so it contributes zero flux at every point.\n\nThe uniform rightward component enters through the left semicircle and exits through the right semicircle. Each small arc on the left has a reflected arc of equal length on the right. The horizontal components of their outward normals are equal in magnitude and opposite in sign. Since the field is uniform, the inward and outward contributions cancel in pairs.\n\nThus the total flux is zero. This does **not** mean the combined field is tangent everywhere: for example, it crosses outward at the rightmost point and inward at the leftmost point.\n\nReversing traversal leaves the outward normals unchanged, so\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\Phi_{-C}=0.\n$$\n\n- The flux across $C$ is positive: **false**.\n- The flux across $-C$ is zero: **true**.\n- The flux across $C$ is negative: **false**.\n\nSelect only the zero-flux statement."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "Check $C_4.$ Its outward normal is $-\\mathbf j,$ giving $g_4=-(x-y)=y-x.$" :answer/id #uuid "5978778d-8f44-562f-bb9b-fe1832be2a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(xy,\\ x-y,\\ -xy,\\ x-y\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717795} {:answer/feedback "The four normals are $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j,-\\mathbf i,-\\mathbf j,$ in the stated order." :answer/id #uuid "ac9fcfd2-0b9a-5c7c-baa0-bb0693ccc4e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(xy,\\ x-y,\\ -xy,\\ y-x\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717800} {:answer/feedback "Check $C_3.$ Its outward normal is $-\\mathbf i,$ giving $g_3=-xy.$" :answer/id #uuid "df333991-97ce-5171-be34-ec9e374a5a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(xy,\\ x-y,\\ xy,\\ y-x\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717805} {:answer/feedback "Check $C_2.$ Its outward normal is $\\mathbf j,$ giving $g_2=x-y.$" :answer/id #uuid "ee464bd5-f89a-55fd-927f-f2deec50b572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(xy,\\ y-x,\\ -xy,\\ y-x\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717806} {:answer/feedback "Check $C_1.$ Its outward normal is $\\mathbf i,$ giving $g_1=xy.$" :answer/id #uuid "f064c49e-4909-5345-a7c7-4fc059350fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(-xy,\\ x-y,\\ -xy,\\ y-x\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717807}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717800} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc88f80e-8f0b-59d2-b5ca-4648cb30419c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "140dea52-1f86-5b7d-8480-0f636b55be31" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=xy\\,\\mathbf i+(x-y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the rectangle $C$ shown above. Its right, top, left, and bottom sides are $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. Write the outward flux in the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_{C_1}g_1\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_2}g_2\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}g_3\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_4}g_4\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich ordered quadruple $(g_1,g_2,g_3,g_4)$ gives the four dot products $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n$? Leave each expression in terms of $x$ and $y$ before substituting side equations. You do not need to evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "Find the outward unit normal separately on each side. The right and left sides have normals $\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf i;$ the top and bottom sides have normals $\\mathbf j$ and $-\\mathbf j.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_1&=\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf i=xy,\\\\\ng_2&=\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf j=x-y,\\\\\ng_3&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf i)=-xy,\\\\\ng_4&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf j)=-(x-y)=y-x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe full flux expression is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_{C_1}xy\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_2}(x-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}(-xy)\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_4}(y-x)\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the correct ordered quadruple is $(xy,x-y,-xy,y-x).$ Opposite-side terms are integrated over different sides, so their displayed signs do not justify canceling the integrals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "The two component products are $2x^2y$ and $-x^2y,$ which sum to $x^2y.$" :answer/id #uuid "63b48c49-d7ea-59d2-95a8-57823d1bab18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x^2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717796} {:answer/feedback "The second component is negative: the contributions are $2x^2y$ and $-x^2y.$" :answer/id #uuid "6fe16d74-0f96-5f4d-aab1-3711e8972f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x^2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717797} {:answer/feedback "Multiply the first component by $x/2$ and the second by $y/2$; the unit normal varies around the circle." :answer/id #uuid "be99c75f-0e6f-5d82-aad8-1441e42d5f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2xy-x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717801} {:answer/feedback "The second product is $(-2x^2)(y/2)=-x^2y.$ Do not interchange the roles of $x$ and $y.$" :answer/id #uuid "daf649ba-ce97-5229-9e67-c990f1a75515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x^2y-xy^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717803} {:answer/feedback "Adding the components of the field alone does not give flux density. Take the dot product with the unit normal." :answer/id #uuid "f995a8a0-4e5c-50d0-94f1-9cc6d492e8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4xy-2x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717796} :answer-field/id #uuid "004197ed-995a-5763-9b09-f813985ba041" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "47d1f8ac-6ef4-5d44-89ba-20fc81520974" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=4xy\\,\\mathbf i-2x^2\\,\\mathbf j$ and the circle $C$ given by $x^2+y^2=4.$ If the outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is written as\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\boxed{\\phantom{x^2y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression? You do not need to evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf n=\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\mathbf i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\mathbf j.$*" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nso the missing expression is the dot product with the supplied unit normal.\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nMultiply corresponding components and add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\n&=(4xy)\\frac{x}{2}+(-2x^2)\\frac{y}{2}\\\\\n&=2x^2y-x^2y\\\\\n&=x^2y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux is $\\oint_C x^2y\\,\\mathrm{d}s,$ and the missing expression is $x^2y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "This side has a horizontal normal. Use the $\\mathbf i$ component rather than the $\\mathbf j$ component." :answer/id #uuid "16736892-e975-5701-ab0c-ab6299261bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717790} {:answer/feedback "This uses the inward normal $\\mathbf i.$ The outward normal on the left side is $-\\mathbf i.$" :answer/id #uuid "4204c696-2e28-5070-93c6-63d3ab8d4e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x^2+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717791} {:answer/feedback "The normal is $-\\mathbf i,$ so negate the entire $\\mathbf i$ component: $-(x^2+y)=-x^2-y.$" :answer/id #uuid "4e827695-774d-5ded-a45e-52670881a835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x^2-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717794} {:answer/feedback "The $\\mathbf j$ component is tangent to the left side; its dot product with the horizontal normal is zero." :answer/id #uuid "9cfb05d5-5d41-5d69-bb15-6989ad862074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717798} {:answer/feedback "The left outward unit normal is $-\\mathbf i,$ so the dot product is $-(x^2+y).$" :answer/id #uuid "c65b7e52-b751-537c-96df-509564360489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x^2-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717802} :answer-field/id #uuid "a18b9f80-6865-552e-9bc0-5d9b64846343" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4e6b2ede-da1d-5e4c-b58b-46040932a2a4" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d9663a0a40f3c3860cce0712e9e9aab41d55251ea15fca33ec663515af57d10.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x^2+y)\\,\\mathbf i+xy\\,\\mathbf j$ and the rectangle $C$ shown above. If the outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across the left side $C_3$ is written as\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_3}\\boxed{\\phantom{x^2+y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression? Leave it in terms of $x$ and $y$ without substituting the equation of the side or evaluating the integral." :question/worked-solution "The integrand is $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n,$ where $\\mathbf n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal. On the left side $C_3,$ outward is to the left, so $\\mathbf n=-\\mathbf i.$\n\n![](images/c6/c6dd493e02633854184be1be0f38246e1e9dce2949f9cc1aa93b943c91d1e44c.png)\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\n&=\\bigl((x^2+y)\\,\\mathbf i+xy\\,\\mathbf j\\bigr)\\cdot(-\\mathbf i)\\\\\n&=-(x^2+y)\\\\\n&=-x^2-y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $\\mathbf j$ component is tangent to this side and contributes zero. Therefore, the required expression is $-x^2-y,$ and the flux through $C_3$ is $\\int_{C_3}(-x^2-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48c8e54e-c8da-501f-a158-0f8245b1e620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717958} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2cdac1-d34e-5c87-8f8f-413f8347adc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717961} {:answer/id #uuid "a55a15ae-eb48-5f4c-be0d-6ad551c403be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717964} {:answer/id #uuid "a58c9b98-1c60-5eed-bed5-fbc5f387956a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717965} {:answer/id #uuid "c5842771-f405-58fb-9388-df9540da3ce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717964} :answer-field/id #uuid "42a8b0c4-a1ab-5e6b-a724-7781b5a2bd43" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2dd4697b-209a-571f-89d3-c852c2e6798d" :question/problem "![Vector field and square boundary](images/c1/c1ddc181913de94b2c95b7b372a5db4124b1d47c6b592aa3195e2ca25208bafd.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=-x\\,\\mathbf i-y\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $2$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes, as shown above. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^4\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n_k$ is the outward-pointing unit normal on side $C_k$.\n\n![Square sides and outward unit normals](images/76/7654d3ef80bdb0e864089738817c440605c84dcd5a9b47bc063b34f8d3c5f3da.png)\n\nFirst consider the right side $C_1$, where $x=1$ and $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$. A parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j,\\qquad -1\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf j$, we have $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=1$ and $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. Along this side,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\n=(-\\mathbf i-t\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf i=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Phi_1=\\int_{-1}^{1}(-1)\\,\\mathrm dt=-2.\n$$\n\nRotating the square and this inward radial field by a quarter-turn preserves the normal component and side length, so each side contributes $-2$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi=4\\Phi_1=4(-2)=-8.\n$$\n\nThe negative sign indicates net inflow. Opposite sides add here: both the field and the outward normal reverse, leaving their dot product unchanged."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d8f152-0b66-5975-b207-c3e71825c003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717953} {:answer/id #uuid "41b6f332-484e-50a5-bf57-270b6edd9cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717956} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc0f6d7-4f46-5900-9308-ad2fe3e4631d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717959} {:answer/id #uuid "caef8b48-67f0-5abd-b4f9-7f37330672aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717967} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3aa4a6-29a8-5f74-b3e0-326fe4a098d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717953} :answer-field/id #uuid "1045fd72-6d04-51e2-80b8-89f8a052d8cd" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "98fefaec-1ff7-5e30-9e14-6167d3e33350" :question/problem "![Vector field and square boundary](images/9b/9bd4a824dff41012a480d6bb56d0ab264138ca660ab3ad0e2e086cf09e5a4e9e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=-\\mathbf i$ and the square $C$ with side length $2$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes, as shown above. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$." :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^4\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n_k$ is the outward-pointing unit normal on side $C_k$.\n\n![Square sides and outward unit normals](images/76/7654d3ef80bdb0e864089738817c440605c84dcd5a9b47bc063b34f8d3c5f3da.png)\n\nThe field is parallel to the top and bottom sides, so its normal component is zero there:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_2=\\Phi_4=0.\n$$\n\nOn the right side, $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$, so $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1=-1$. On the left side, $\\mathbf n_3=-\\mathbf i$, so $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3=1$. Each side has length $2$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi_1=(-1)(2)=-2,\\qquad \\Phi_3=(1)(2)=2.\n$$\n\nThe inflow on the right cancels the equal outflow on the left. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\Phi=-2+0+2+0=0.\n$$\n\nZero total flux does not mean the field has zero flow through every side."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbf5576b-004c-5e3b-97df-bf5db1e7bdb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717968} :answer-field/id #uuid "788aa9e0-c1c2-5f19-8907-ac2b9187b3b8" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a0c9c202-a2fa-5748-86e8-70222d236bed" :question/problem "![Vector field and square boundary](images/54/54d522ffa63b12cbf84269753bfe641735997ecc86874c77adfa26e3ef0898be.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(x-2)\\,\\mathbf i+(y+1)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $2$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes, as shown above. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^4\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n_k$ is the outward-pointing unit normal on side $C_k$.\n\n![Square sides and outward unit normals](images/76/7654d3ef80bdb0e864089738817c440605c84dcd5a9b47bc063b34f8d3c5f3da.png)\n\nThe full field does not give equal flux through all four sides. Instead, separate it into a radial field and a uniform field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F=\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U,\\qquad\n\\mathbf R=x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j,\\qquad\n\\mathbf U=-2\\,\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nSince $(\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U)\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\mathbf R\\cdot\\mathbf n+\\mathbf U\\cdot\\mathbf n$, their flux contributions add.\n\nFor the uniform field $\\mathbf U$, opposite sides have opposite normals and equal lengths. Its right, top, left and bottom contributions are $-4$, $2$, $4$ and $-2$, respectively. These cancel, so $\\Phi_{\\mathbf U}=0$.\n\nFor the radial field $\\mathbf R$, parametrize the right side by $\\mathbf r(t)=\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-1\\le t\\le1$. Here $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=1$, giving\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf R\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\int_{-1}^{1}(\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf i\\,\\mathrm dt\n=\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\mathrm dt=2.\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry gives the same radial contribution on every side. Hence\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf R}=4(2)=8,\\qquad \\Phi=\\Phi_{\\mathbf R}+\\Phi_{\\mathbf U}=8+0=8.\n$$\n\nAs a check, the full field has the following contributions:\n\n| Side | $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n$ | Flux |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Right | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n| Top | $2$ | $4$ |\n| Left | $3$ | $6$ |\n| Bottom | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nThese sum to $-2+4+6+0=8$. Multiplying the full field's right-side flux by four would be invalid; the fourfold symmetry applies to $\\mathbf R$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14212e2d-99d1-5858-a3e7-25419e39747c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718109} {:answer/id #uuid "db06fa59-7deb-5237-8b8e-cf4a2dd3d17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718145}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718145} :answer-field/id #uuid "3781c3bd-84c4-5eee-89e8-9908be684fab" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718117} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab8526e-c81d-5116-8d9c-772b1bb811b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718114} {:answer/id #uuid "bac56a90-b845-54c8-a1f6-f92ceaf8371a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718139}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718114} :answer-field/id #uuid "77e92646-2dae-55d0-bbac-3f286c347f65" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is nonzero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718131} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16228aa1-cbf5-518a-9c7a-98a1432f189c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718110} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0b3da9-5f8d-5ab3-9530-4200fc8b7195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718153} :answer-field/id #uuid "95d13688-49bc-569d-90c5-f43f5f4bebc7" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "108b02be-5f1f-592c-97db-d40bb44d687b" :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/01/015f414e673c7484833a3338ed6520ddc5f7d7e212643c5d0e3dff03978c0186.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and boundary $S$ shown above. $\\mathbf F$ expands horizontally and compresses vertically, at speeds proportional to distance from the corresponding axis. The expansion and compression rates are equal.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once clockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nSplit $\\mathbf F$ into its horizontal and vertical flows. On the circle, pair equal-length small arcs related by a quarter turn. Their horizontal and vertical normal projections have matching geometric weights. Their normal contributions are equal and opposite, so they cancel. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ is zero even though the field crosses the boundary.\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/f9/f9643ae31e16b22cf0b82d817a3e473bcf404b66287c9cad6fda352da1314bcb.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C=0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is nonzero. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive. **false**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "078c4ad6-1db7-5074-a437-6f14cf79d58c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718105} {:answer/id #uuid "0e0df513-8537-580d-bb59-c84986a68881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718107}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718105} :answer-field/id #uuid "18ad31dd-1764-5150-9546-bb123ba2678f" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718111} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6efe5ab3-ec05-5209-8f79-d3adb75e80c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718127} {:answer/id #uuid "c88ccd23-9085-5450-a386-c6a5e58cfec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718142}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718142} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cd6d3e9-0736-574d-9dc1-8f22c648e326" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718132} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49b24c83-d1fa-5b82-b491-4abe94512fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718121} {:answer/id #uuid "aeac2b06-14fa-5852-bcd8-0d4d10641c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718121} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8d52d53-be7b-5354-af15-0ab59f08f52a" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is nonzero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "451b0446-ab81-5796-9b2a-c82d74953847" :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/ee/ee900d037687ed35815a9cc5753cd829c014f307c970eeea91342c7aa2d743c9.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and boundary $S$ shown above. The blue boundary and gray reference curves are concentric circles. Boundary crossing directions and tangencies shown by the arrows hold between the sampled points as well.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once counterclockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nThe field has motion along the boundary, but its arrows also cross consistently inward. Tangential motion contributes no flux. The normal component is negative all around the boundary, so the flux of $\\mathbf F$ is negative.\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/01/01f72b9b0266cb8c3cd36ada6aac37050fee3239735c485dd5d3d7ba5347ef97.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C<0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is nonzero. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative. **true**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5044525e-4e35-5090-b79f-52b45b50f1b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718122} {:answer/id #uuid "fe40490f-4e6f-53c8-b8d3-28b8b823faa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718154}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718122} :answer-field/id #uuid "279d367e-36ad-5f4d-8e10-481e3930b774" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7497f429-fbd6-5524-99a4-18ea3a7b2027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718129} {:answer/id #uuid "8456fe1b-1fff-5f58-a053-e5f217101bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718133} :answer-field/id #uuid "2abfc289-b935-5852-8bc3-247198d82ce5" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is nonzero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718115} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7431dba0-0474-5f8b-b56d-4266c1e7fa59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718128} {:answer/id #uuid "e34ae0b5-10fb-564b-b8b9-dff624f0ef54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718146}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718146} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed156e0e-874f-5b02-913d-87d5dc44eaa1" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a9ec168-5ec4-5dea-8cd7-1d51b0c09124" :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/c0/c09f161967ce1a816fa8ac6003358594a9d409a56d6480a730003cd2fcd4f38c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and boundary $S$ shown above. $\\mathbf F$ expands horizontally and compresses vertically, at speeds proportional to distance from the corresponding axis. The expansion rate is three times the other rate.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once counterclockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nSplit $\\mathbf F$ into its horizontal and vertical flows. On the circle, pair equal-length small arcs related by a quarter turn. Their horizontal and vertical normal projections have matching geometric weights. The expanding contribution is three times as large as the opposing contribution. There is net outflow, so the flux of $\\mathbf F$ is positive.\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/37/37d4431e42b8defeb89e4d0d241aa444459f79902c6b7eaf454397703e09d76b.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C>0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is nonzero. **true**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4668e574-638d-50bd-9e2f-e28553f254e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718119} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ddf84d-efda-5ac7-aadb-b00ca8c17396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718119} :answer-field/id #uuid "518c6250-15aa-591b-97bf-84739102b42d" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is not negative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718123} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd17f70-e3cb-5067-bb02-5900dce91827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718106} {:answer/id #uuid "33950bfc-0bc4-5c2c-8942-40be331386eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718116}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718116} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d58c864-77e7-54be-8d62-6dbf02573a16" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718135} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a08aaeab-c799-589f-a6de-9d5d57597394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718137} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd5449c-1d54-5082-b054-8350c3a5396e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718140} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef853b3c-67c7-5061-a847-5ce1c0ad1259" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is nonzero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f9fb36af-51b3-5500-b76b-d377bfba0a44" :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/8f/8f473f1958f39173a66ded9caf1da1bb47efaeb0b3f2874b0763baf007603c4b.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and boundary $S$ shown above. The blue boundary and gray reference curves are concentric similar ellipses. Boundary crossing directions and tangencies shown by the arrows hold between the sampled points as well.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once clockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nEvery boundary vector of $\\mathbf F$ is tangent to the boundary, hence perpendicular to the outward normal. Thus $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n=0$ at every point, and its flux is zero.\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/00/00d37bd512a3e54c0152f3a36eb85028ce26811e7b9f6221fed730a74ac2c209.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C=0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is nonzero. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is not negative. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative. **false**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60d3b2d2-32cc-516d-94ad-1e2bc3e429a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718125} {:answer/id #uuid "7740853a-45a2-574d-af9c-e01ad8867473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718125} :answer-field/id #uuid "48eb9f89-66ca-5f33-810c-f2547c78ce40" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is negative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718120} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cd632a7-8492-5ae7-8a7f-3564eead89c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718112} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f335a3-0863-5333-a494-a7ec06fb6576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718150}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718150} :answer-field/id #uuid "55c51808-fd11-5cf7-bdee-048c53407a0c" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is not negative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718124} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7828b1d-157d-540b-9b64-6206c5859aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718141} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a30561-0cd8-527e-b6f6-c5ca074db60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718144}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718144} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b9ab73f-f38c-5d31-ab28-e014ea132e3f" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fa9468bd-7db8-5511-a2e7-8d74c72e03c0" :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/fb/fb36ae15e8e246c28658a4aec287b0e729af42f22a750a48985d2cdac052684a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and boundary $S$ shown above. $\\mathbf F$ expands horizontally and compresses vertically, at speeds proportional to distance from the corresponding axis. The compression rate is three times the other rate.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once counterclockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nSplit $\\mathbf F$ into its horizontal and vertical flows. On the circle, pair equal-length small arcs related by a quarter turn. Their horizontal and vertical normal projections have matching geometric weights. The compressing contribution is three times as large as the opposing contribution. There is net inflow, so the flux of $\\mathbf F$ is negative.\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/3b/3bfe8d84d85f76649e80e7a3d285454a4ee3fa08d9d7ac055d26fd31ec4bf845.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C<0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is not negative. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is negative. **true**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718165 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b4165e6-1b37-5ec9-a725-42ac3dd65b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718156} {:answer/id #uuid "26b6d1a3-243e-5d6a-bdb4-bdc932dee53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718156} :answer-field/id #uuid "301f4aba-f0a2-5a43-bc38-af1e325b8552" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718161} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7e53850-a6ed-55d9-b9d7-cba3e6139584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718185} {:answer/id #uuid "e1965893-2cf1-5292-87f0-29aa43bc5fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718189}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718189} :answer-field/id #uuid "76c02dbd-9a5a-50e1-a854-e5d2c0f09102" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718170} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efa651d2-7cf4-57f9-85f7-d9718cd167ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718194} {:answer/id #uuid "fed02aef-1915-5632-ab6d-4421edfb9d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718195}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718194} :answer-field/id #uuid "b12563d7-2591-51f4-8942-68fd81669c4e" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is positive." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "551a510a-d03b-5b49-ab39-222613c3e782" :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/53/53f7c22a46869946a6ca502433b53ff18c5a08305ba2d7c0b6b70c003be6d40e.png)\n\nThe vector field is $\\mathbf F=\\mathbf U+\\mathbf V$. The panels show the two components separately at the same arrow scale, on the same boundary $S$. The field $\\mathbf U$ is uniform throughout the plane. The blue boundary and gray reference curves are concentric circles. Boundary crossing directions and tangencies shown by the arrows hold between the sampled points as well.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once counterclockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nFor the uniform field $\\mathbf U$, opposite small boundary arcs have equal lengths and opposite outward normals. The same field vector meets both arcs, so their normal contributions cancel in pairs. Thus $\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)=0$.\n\nThe field has motion along the boundary, but its arrows also cross consistently inward. Tangential motion contributes no flux. The normal component is negative all around the boundary, so the flux of $\\mathbf V$ is negative.\n\nThe normal component of a sum is the sum of its normal components. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi_C(\\mathbf F)&=\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V)\\\\\n&=0+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/45/45032959530381081b44ac1039705a73b528f6c233219241e53d8665b4c8b0cf.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C<0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is positive. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero. **false**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24d5dc97-61cc-501e-94b3-e20fefe3ed71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718158} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f59099-93ad-5386-b9a2-0235073d7be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718158} :answer-field/id #uuid "b271c828-5843-5fac-8134-a059e85f80f0" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is not negative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718179} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1aeee16-6260-57e8-a41a-0b3fa9a01b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718186} {:answer/id #uuid "d622deb5-a40a-5f81-9a85-62487f58e782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718187}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718186} :answer-field/id #uuid "b979a9d6-654b-5769-bd92-8ea74cb7e2ae" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is nonzero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718182} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b9638f8-0231-515c-9f52-84eb50617111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718171} {:answer/id #uuid "db14da94-f7a3-57a1-a241-62fd3fe6e612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718188} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2f4ff7b-593e-567f-b4d7-d701be117f93" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "61f7e928-c8f0-5cf8-bea9-8f07072c3eb7" :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/8d/8d83151eeb2676fe98c843d5875f966352da6302e81078c6f41a304f72cef4e9.png)\n\nThe vector field is $\\mathbf F=\\mathbf U+\\mathbf V$. The panels show the two components separately at the same arrow scale, on the same boundary $S$. The field $\\mathbf U$ is uniform throughout the plane. $\\mathbf V$ compresses horizontally and expands vertically, at speeds proportional to distance from the corresponding axis. The expansion rate is three times the other rate.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once clockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nFor the uniform field $\\mathbf U$, opposite small boundary arcs have equal lengths and opposite outward normals. The same field vector meets both arcs, so their normal contributions cancel in pairs. Thus $\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)=0$.\n\nSplit $\\mathbf V$ into its horizontal and vertical flows. On the circle, pair equal-length small arcs related by a quarter turn. Their horizontal and vertical normal projections have matching geometric weights. The expanding contribution is three times as large as the opposing contribution. There is net outflow, so the flux of $\\mathbf V$ is positive.\n\nThe normal component of a sum is the sum of its normal components. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi_C(\\mathbf F)&=\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V)\\\\\n&=0+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/1c/1cc81aae3e10bd3d1f1b0636cc3bfc14cdedaf36077999311f690ac3ddb5de3f.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C>0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is not negative. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is nonzero. **true**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53e4881a-1f69-5da7-a36b-7a661f1fb155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718164} {:answer/id #uuid "74622cb4-a318-55ca-a283-bf59f4763e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718169}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718169} :answer-field/id #uuid "37983ed9-bb95-57ea-8ff4-01c47cb6f761" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is nonzero." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718162} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c227639-b586-5d70-95aa-ba6df9e5f05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718173} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a98def-7094-5a6f-b186-b075be37b17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718180} :answer-field/id #uuid "58620992-6c42-5fba-b599-94049f79ba32" :answer-field/key "The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is not negative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20f4e9d2-7e29-59e2