rd ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nFor the uniform field $\\mathbf U$, opposite small boundary arcs have equal lengths and opposite outward normals. The same field vector meets both arcs, so their normal contributions cancel in pairs. Thus $\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)=0$.\n\nSplit $\\mathbf V$ into its horizontal and vertical flows. On the circle, pair equal-length small arcs related by a quarter turn. Their horizontal and vertical normal projections have matching geometric weights. Their normal contributions are equal and opposite, so they cancel. The flux of $\\mathbf V$ is zero even though the field crosses the boundary.\n\nThe normal component of a sum is the sum of its normal components. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi_C(\\mathbf F)&=\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V)\\\\\n&=0+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/89/894a06c475fd9611f4d335d5d26560ddd784b6c82f4fc23633607dbdb4a5a0a9.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C=0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is nonzero. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is not positive. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive. **false**.\n"}]}
